From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:42:15 +0300
Subject: SDL video: the 32-bit buffer in the texture's pixel format

The software renderer's frame goes 8-bit screen -> palette blit into `buffer`
(SDL_CreateRGBSurface depth 32 with zero masks: SDL_PIXELFORMAT_XRGB8888) ->
SDL_UpdateTexture(sdl_texture, ..., buffer->pixels) every frame. sdl_texture comes from
SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(renderer, buffer), which may choose a format the renderer prefers,
while SDL_UpdateTexture takes pixels in the texture's format. On little-endian the choice
(XRGB8888 or ARGB8888) has XRGB8888's byte layout, so it worked by accident. On big-endian,
SDL3's (sdl2-compat's) GL and GLES2 renderers list only byte-order formats (BGRA32, RGBA32,
BGRX32, RGBX32), and so does the GL-backed window framebuffer SDL's software renderer draws
into under X11: there is no XRGB8888, and the texture becomes BGRX8888, bytes B,G,R,X. The
buffer's XRGB8888 bytes X,R,G,B were read as B,G,R,X: red showed as green, green as red, and
blue was replaced by the padding byte (0), with every SDL renderer (the PS3).

Remake the buffer in the texture's format, so the palette blit (which maps the palette into
any destination format) writes exactly what the texture expects, at no cost per frame; if that
format can't be a 32-bit surface, create the texture in the buffer's format instead and let
SDL convert. The palette's alpha is set (opaque) instead of left uninitialised, for textures
chosen with an alpha channel.

Forwarded: no

diff --git a/prboom2/src/SDL/i_video.c b/prboom2/src/SDL/i_video.c
--- a/prboom2/src/SDL/i_video.c
+++ b/prboom2/src/SDL/i_video.c
@@ -553,6 +553,7 @@ static void I_UploadNewPalette(int pal, int force)
       playpal_data->colours[i].r = gtable[palette[0]];
       playpal_data->colours[i].g = gtable[palette[1]];
       playpal_data->colours[i].b = gtable[palette[2]];
+      playpal_data->colours[i].a = SDL_ALPHA_OPAQUE;
       palette += 3;
     }
 
@@ -1322,6 +1323,43 @@ void I_UpdateVideoMode(void)
 
     sdl_texture = SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(sdl_renderer, buffer);
 
+    // SDL_UpdateTexture (I_FinishUpdate) takes pixels in the texture's format,
+    // which SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface may pick differently from the buffer's
+    // (XRGB8888): on big-endian, SDL's GL and GLES2 renderers and its
+    // GL-backed window framebuffer have no XRGB8888 textures and choose
+    // BGRX8888, whose bytes (B,G,R,X) are XRGB8888's (X,R,G,B) in another
+    // order. Make the buffer that format, so the palette blit writes what the
+    // texture expects; otherwise give the texture the buffer's format and let
+    // SDL convert.
+    if (sdl_texture && buffer)
+    {
+      Uint32 format;
+
+      if (SDL_QueryTexture(sdl_texture, &format, NULL, NULL, NULL) == 0 &&
+          format != buffer->format->format)
+      {
+        SDL_Surface *matching = NULL;
+
+        if (SDL_BYTESPERPIXEL(format) == 4 &&
+            !SDL_ISPIXELFORMAT_INDEXED(format) && !SDL_ISPIXELFORMAT_FOURCC(format))
+          matching = SDL_CreateRGBSurfaceWithFormat(0, SCREENWIDTH, SCREENHEIGHT, 32, format);
+
+        if (matching)
+        {
+          SDL_FreeSurface(buffer);
+          buffer = matching;
+          SDL_FillRect(buffer, NULL, 0);
+        }
+        else
+        {
+          SDL_DestroyTexture(sdl_texture);
+          sdl_texture = SDL_CreateTexture(sdl_renderer, buffer->format->format,
+                                          SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING,
+                                          SCREENWIDTH, SCREENHEIGHT);
+        }
+      }
+    }
+
     if(screen == NULL) {
       I_Error("Couldn't set %dx%d video mode [%s]", SCREENWIDTH, SCREENHEIGHT, SDL_GetError());
     }
